╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ zero_lag_quadratic_endpoint_filter                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current exponential-weighted moving average induces significant lag (0.38 lag error)  │
│                          │ and many spurious slope changes (223) because it's a causal smoother whose effective      │
│                          │ center of mass sits well behind the newest sample. A far better approach for the four     │
│                          │ objectives: fit a local quadratic (or linear, rank-deficient-safe) polynomial to each     │
│                          │ window via least squares and evaluate it at the *most recent* window sample. This is a    │
│                          │ Savitzky-Golay-style endpoint estimator: (1) it has near-zero phase/lag because the fit   │
│                          │ extrapolates to the window's leading edge rather than averaging behind it; (2) the        │
│                          │ quadratic term captures local curvature so genuine trends and turning points are          │
│                          │ preserved (tracking accuracy); (3) polynomial least squares acts as a low-pass projection │
│                          │ that strongly suppresses uncorrelated noise (noise reduction, smoothness); (4) the        │
│                          │ estimated local slope from the fitted derivative is far more stable than finite           │
│                          │ differences of a noisy average, drastically reducing false reversals and slope changes.   │
│                          │ To make it robust, I: (a) fit the quadratic with scaled time coordinates for numerical    │
│                          │ conditioning, (b) detect when a window is in a fast-varying regime and shrink the         │
│                          │ effective window (adaptive windowing) to avoid over-smoothing genuine dynamics, and (c)   │
│                          │ fall back to a linear fit when the quadratic system is ill-conditioned. I keep the same   │
│                          │ output length and function signatures so the rest of the program (alignment via `delay`,  │
│                          │ metrics) still works unchanged.                                                           │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
