│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_kalman_trend_filter                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The original program uses simple/exponentially-weighted moving averages, which introduce  │
│                          │ fixed lag proportional to window size and pass noise-induced slope reversals. My novel    │
│                          │ approach replaces fixed smoothing windows with a two-state (level + slope) Kalman filter  │
│                          │ running causally over the samples, then reports window-anchored outputs (same output      │
│                          │ length/alignment as the sliding-window baseline). Key insights: (1) an explicit slope     │
│                          │ state lets the filter *predict* the next sample from the current trend, drastically       │
│                          │ reducing lag error versus backward-looking averages; (2) adaptive process noise           │
│                          │ (innovation-based, like adaptive Kalman/Sage-Husa estimators) makes the filter            │
│                          │ automatically tighten smoothing in quiet regions and react fast during genuine regime     │
│                          │ changes; (3) a slope-hysteresis smoothing stage suppresses false reversals by requiring   │
│                          │ the estimated slope to persistently exceed a noise-scaled deadzone before contributing to │
│                          │ the output. This combines Kalman trend tracking with a trend-detection/hysteresis         │
│                          │ paradigm — fundamentally different from the weighted-average approach while keeping       │
│                          │ identical interface (process_signal, run_signal_processing, etc.).                        │
