│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ reversal_suppression_postpass                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The metrics show the current filter tracks well (correlation 0.92, lag 0.36) but suffers  │
│                          │ from many slope changes (231) and false reversals (183), giving a very low smoothness     │
│                          │ score (0.08). The bottleneck is spurious high-frequency ripple in the endpoint estimates, │
│                          │ not lag. Adding more linear smoothing would increase lag error, so instead I add a        │
│                          │ nonlinear "reversal suppression" post-pass: small sign changes in the output's first      │
│                          │ difference that are within the estimated noise floor are damped (partially reverted       │
│                          │ toward the previous trend), while large, genuine reversals (magnitude well above noise)   │
│                          │ are passed through untouched. This directly targets slope-change minimization and the     │
│                          │ false-reversal penalty with almost zero additional lag, since only sub-noise-scale        │
│                          │ reversals are attenuated. The noise floor is estimated robustly via MAD of the first      │
│                          │ differences already computed, so the method adapts to the signal's noise level without    │
│                          │ parameters tied to a specific test signal.                                                │
