│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ zero_phase_kalman_savgol_filter                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the causal weighted moving average (which introduces lag and phase delay) with a  │
│                          │ hybrid zero-phase filter: (1) a 1st-order adaptive Kalman filter whose measurement noise  │
│                          │ R is estimated online from local residual variance, giving strong noise reduction with    │
│                          │ responsiveness; (2) a short Savitzky-Golay smoothing pass that preserves polynomial       │
│                          │ trends (slope) without lag (centered window); (3) an optional endpoint extension          │
│                          │ (reflect-padding) so output length matches input length, drastically reducing lag error   │
│                          │ since no samples are dropped. The Kalman gain adapts: high local noise → more smoothing   │
│                          │ (fewer false reversals/slope changes); low noise → responsive tracking. Savgol preserves  │
│                          │ slopes (trend dynamics), reducing slope changes and false reversals while keeping         │
│                          │ tracking accuracy. All operations are O(N), keeping efficiency high.                      │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
