│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_kalman_postfilter                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The weakest metrics are smoothness (0.09), slope changes (198) and false reversals (156)  │
│                          │ — the filtered output still flips direction too often, while noise reduction (0.41) also  │
│                          │ lags. The current post-processing (fixed one-pole smoother + crude reversal damping)      │
│                          │ cannot jointly fix these: the fixed alpha either over-smooths (lag) or under-smooths      │
│                          │ (ripple).                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ A constant-velocity (local-linear) Kalman filter applied to the zero-lag endpoint         │
│                          │ estimates is the principled fix: it optimally fuses the noisy per-window endpoint         │
│                          │ estimates with a smoothness prior, and by adapting the measurement-noise covariance R     │
│                          │ from the robust residual scale (r_std) and the process-noise covariance Q from the local  │
│                          │ signal dynamics, it automatically becomes more smoothing when the signal is quiet         │
│                          │ (killing noise-driven slope flips) and more responsive when the signal is genuinely       │
│                          │ moving (keeping lag error small). The Kalman gain is the closed-form solution to the      │
│                          │ exact multi-objective trade-off we need: it minimizes expected deviation (lag/accuracy)   │
│                          │ while the implicit state model suppresses sub-noise reversals far more effectively than   │
│                          │ the heuristic damping loop, because the velocity state carries trend memory across        │
│                          │ samples.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This replaces both the one-pole smoother and the reversal-suppression hack with a single  │
│                          │ statistically-grounded recursion, keeping output contract identical.                      │
