│ patch_name               │ savgol_zerophase_trend_filter                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of a causal Kalman/EMA approach, this uses a globally-applied Savitzky-Golay      │
│                          │ local polynomial (least-squares) filter, which is zero-phase: it fits low-order           │
│                          │ polynomials in a moving window and evaluates at the window's leading edge, giving         │
│                          │ trend-preserving smoothing with essentially no lag (group delay ~0) for signals           │
│                          │ well-approximated locally by cubics. Since the function receives the entire signal, we    │
│                          │ can exploit future samples within each window — unlike the causal Kalman filter which     │
│                          │ must wait for evidence and thus lags. A noise-adaptive component blends the polynomial    │
│                          │ estimate with a robust (Hampel/median) estimate to suppress outliers, and a               │
│                          │ slope-consistency pass with hysteresis eliminates spurious directional reversals. This    │
│                          │ directly targets: lag error (zero-phase fit), tracking accuracy (cubic captures sin       │
│                          │ dynamics), smoothness/slope changes (least-squares polynomial is smooth, hysteresis kills │
│                          │ noise reversals).                                                                         │
