│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hybrid_hysteresis_trend_filter                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ Inspired by the prior top performers: the zero-lag quadratic endpoint fit (from program   │
│                          │ 3/4) gives near-zero lag and high accuracy, but all prior versions still suffer ~150-200  │
│                          │ slope changes and false reversals with poor smoothness. The key insight: the missing      │
│                          │ ingredient is not more smoothing (which hurts lag), but *decision-level trend hysteresis* │
│                          │ — a discrete trend state machine that only commits to a slope reversal when evidence is   │
│                          │ sustained and exceeds a robust noise floor. This design combines: (1) a vectorized        │
│                          │ zero-lag polynomial endpoint estimator with adaptive window shrinking for fast regimes,   │
│                          │ (2) a constant-velocity Kalman filter whose process noise adapts to the local signal      │
│                          │ dynamics, and (3) a novel hysteresis trend gate that separates the *internal* (accurate)  │
│                          │ estimate from the *emitted* trend: sign flips in the output slope are only released after │
│                          │ K consecutive confirmations or when the reversal magnitude exceeds 2× the MAD-estimated   │
│                          │ noise floor. This cuts spurious reversals at the source while preserving genuine dynamics │
│                          │ and minimal lag, since large, genuine reversals pass through immediately.                 │
