│ patch_name               │ kalman_trend_adaptive_filter                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ A fundamentally different approach: instead of sliding-window polynomial fitting, this    │
│                          │ uses a two-state Kalman filter (level + slope) with an adaptive process-noise scheduler   │
│                          │ driven by a trend-consistency detector. The Kalman filter inherently minimizes lag        │
│                          │ because it is predictive (the slope state carries the signal forward one step). Lag error │
│                          │ is further canceled by running the filter forward, then re-running it backward over a     │
│                          │ short lookback window and fusing via weighted blend that is exact for linear trends       │
│                          │ (zero-lag for ramps). Slope-change spurious reversals are suppressed by the adaptive Q    │
│                          │ matrix: when the last K slope signs agree, process noise on the slope collapses, making   │
│                          │ the filter resist direction changes (smooth trends); when a persistent innovation         │
│                          │ (prediction error) exceeds a Mahalanobis threshold, Q is inflated so the filter snaps to  │
│                          │ genuine reversals quickly. This directly targets all four objectives with O(1) per-sample │
│                          │ cost (no window refits), giving both low latency and low reversal count.                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
