│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fused_velocity_deadband_filter                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ Builds on the strongest prior program (Gen with 0.57): the adaptive quadratic endpoint    │
│                          │ fit plus zero-lag bidirectional one-pole smoothing, which already reduced slope changes   │
│                          │ to ~100 and false reversals to ~77. Per the recommendation, the noisy per-sample          │
│                          │ reversal-damping loop (which can distort genuine dynamics) is replaced by a single        │
│                          │ lightweight local mechanism: a deadband applied directly to the fused                     │
│                          │ bidirectional-filtered velocity. The fused velocity is computed from forward- and         │
│                          │ backward-pass state differences (vel_f, vel_b), combined, then shrunk toward zero by a    │
│                          │ noise-adaptive threshold (~0.2 × std of the input's first differences). Where the         │
│                          │ deadband zeroes the velocity, the level is corrected to stay on the prior value,          │
│                          │ eliminating spurious micro-reversals without an extra pipeline stage, while genuine large │
│                          │ slope changes pass through untouched so lag error and responsiveness remain intact.       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
