│ patch_name               │ ewma_innovation_kalman_dualpass                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ This design draws from the prior Kalman-based adaptive filters but fixes their core       │
│                          │ weakness: raw squared innovations fed directly into the measurement noise `r` cause noisy │
│                          │ feedback that produced ~300 slope changes and ~240 false reversals (smoothness 0.06). The │
│                          │ key insight is to decouple the *tracking machinery* (which already achieves correlation   │
│                          │ 0.96 and lag 0.22) from the *noise adaptation machinery*. Instead of `r = r0*0.5 +        │
│                          │ innov²*0.5`, we smooth the squared innovation with a slow EWMA (alpha=0.1) so the         │
│                          │ measurement noise adapts to the *statistical envelope* of the noise rather than reacting  │
│                          │ instantaneously to each sample. This preserves genuine rapid signal movements (large      │
│                          │ innovations from real dynamics don't blow up r and lag the filter) while still adapting   │
│                          │ to changing noise regimes. We keep a dual-pass forward/backward structure for zero phase  │
│                          │ delay, velocity-state augmentation for responsiveness, and a slow trend detector to veto  │
│                          │ spurious slope reversals.                                                                 │
