│ patch_name               │ noise_adaptive_multismooth_hysteresis                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current program's weakest metrics are smoothness (0.23), slope changes (66.8) and     │
│                          │ false reversals (48.0), while lag error (0.28) and correlation (0.96) are strong —        │
│                          │ meaning we have lag budget to spend on smoothness. Since the bidirectional one-pole       │
│                          │ passes are inherently zero-lag (forward+backward passes cancel phase), adding a third,    │
│                          │ milder pass increases smoothing depth nearly for free in lag. Additionally, making the    │
│                          │ smoothing strength noise-adaptive (stronger smoothing when the local residual variance is │
│                          │ high relative to the estimated measurement noise, weaker when the signal is moving        │
│                          │ coherently) prevents over-smoothing genuine dynamics — protecting tracking accuracy and   │
│                          │ false-reversal behavior. Finally, a noise-scaled slope deadzone (suppressing output       │
│                          │ increments whose magnitude falls below a fraction of the estimated noise-induced step)    │
│                          │ directly attacks spurious slope changes: tiny jitter steps are flattened while real trend │
│                          │ steps pass through. This preserves the validated structure and interface, only modifying  │
│                          │ the post-processing pipeline in enhanced_filter_with_trend_preservation plus a small      │
│                          │ helper.                                                                                   │
