│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ widen_only_adaptive_r                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current adaptive R blend (0.5·R0 + 0.5·ewma_var) slightly hurt noise_reduction (0.59  │
│                          │ vs 0.63) and correlation (0.92 vs 0.93) versus the static-R Gen 20 baseline, because when │
│                          │ the EWMA variance dips below R0 (clean segments where innovations are small largely       │
│                          │ because the prediction is good), R tightens, the gain rises, and more measurement noise   │
│                          │ leaks into the estimate — the exact opposite of what we want in quiet regions. The        │
│                          │ theoretically sound use of innovation-based adaptive R in a smoother context is           │
│                          │ *widen-only*: let R increase during genuine noise bursts (large squared innovations ⇒     │
│                          │ wider R ⇒ smaller Kalman gain ⇒ more smoothing) but never fall below the robust static    │
│                          │ MAD estimate R0, which already represents the best global noise floor. I implement this   │
│                          │ as Rk = max(R0, 0.7·R0 + 0.3·ewma_var) in the forward pass, keeping the state update      │
│                          │ purely x_est = xp + K·innov (no signal-shaping terms, per the Gen 2 lesson). The second   │
│                          │ (refinement) pass reuses the same Rseq so it stays consistent. This should recover the    │
│                          │ noise reduction of the static-R version while retaining burst robustness, improving       │
│                          │ smoothness and slope-change/false-reversal metrics with no extra pipeline stage and       │
│                          │ negligible cost.                                                                          │
