│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ ewma_adaptive_r_kalman                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ Builds on the Gen-31 style bidirectional constant-velocity Kalman fusion (high accuracy,  │
│                          │ correlation 0.93) and improves noise reduction — the weakest unsaturated metric — by      │
│                          │ replacing the instantaneous innovation-based R with an EWMA-smoothed measurement-noise    │
│                          │ variance: `R = 0.5·σ_r² + 0.5·ewma_var`, `ewma_var = 0.9·ewma_var + 0.1·innov²`. This     │
│                          │ widens R during noisy bursts (heavier smoothing exactly where noise dominates) and        │
│                          │ tightens it during clean segments (preserving dynamics and low lag), without adding any   │
│                          │ pipeline stage. The state update keeps the pure Kalman form `x_est = xp + K·innov` with   │
│                          │ the symmetrized covariance update — no trend-lean or signal-shaping terms, which prior    │
│                          │ generations showed poison the output. Post-processing retains the zero-lag bidirectional  │
│                          │ one-pole smoothers plus the noise-scaled slope deadzone, which directly reduce slope      │
│                          │ changes and false reversals. Key parameters: EWMA forgetting factor 0.9, 50/50 blend      │
│                          │ between static σ_r² and adaptive ewma_var, Q = (0.02, 0.005), deadzone threshold 0.5·σ_r  │
│                          │ with 20% leak-through, bidirectional one-pole alphas (0.35–0.70 SNR-adaptive, then +0.15, │
│                          │ then 0.75). This targets pushing noise_reduction from 0.54 toward saturation while        │
│                          │ keeping lag_error low and slope changes low.                                              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
