│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ savgol_post_smoother                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current pipeline's bottleneck is noise reduction (0.61) and average error (0.37)      │
│                          │ while slope changes (29) and false reversals (23) are already very low, and lag is small  │
│                          │ (0.33). This means the smoother is being slightly under-smoothed: there is residual noise │
│                          │ left in the output that costs accuracy without helping responsiveness. A zero-phase       │
│                          │ Savitzky–Golay (local polynomial) post-filter is the ideal complement to the RTS          │
│                          │ smoother: it fits low-order polynomials in symmetric windows, so it adds no phase delay,  │
│                          │ preserves local trends (quadratic dynamics pass through exactly), and removes white       │
│                          │ residual noise more effectively than a one-pole pass. Applying it after the bilateral     │
│                          │ one-pole pass, with a window scaled to the estimated noise level, should improve noise    │
│                          │ reduction, correlation and smoothness while leaving lag and slope-change metrics          │
│                          │ essentially unchanged. I also widen the adaptive one-pole alpha floor slightly (0.55 →    │
│                          │ 0.50) since the SG stage now handles trend-preserving denoising.                          │
