│ patch_description        │ This replaces the Kalman/RTS pipeline with a fundamentally different paradigm: a robust,  │
│                          │ adaptively-weighted Whittaker–Henderson penalized least-squares smoother. It minimizes    │
│                          │ ||W^(1/2)(y − x)||² + λ·Σ w_i·(Δ²y)² in one global solve — a discrete smoothing spline.   │
│                          │ Unlike the sequential Kalman approach, this is zero-phase by construction (no             │
│                          │ forward/backward asymmetry), has provably minimal curvature for a given data-fidelity,    │
│                          │ and directly attacks the weakest metrics: noise reduction and slope stability (the        │
│                          │ second-difference penalty is exactly a spurious-slope-change suppressor). Adaptivity      │
│                          │ comes from (1) λ calibrated from robust MAD noise/dynamics estimates, (2) iteratively     │
│                          │ reweighted residuals (asymmetric Huber weights that down-weight noise bursts while        │
│                          │ keeping genuine signal excursions), and (3) a local penalty modulation that relaxes       │
│                          │ smoothing where the underlying signal has genuine curvature (detected via a robust second │
│                          │ difference), preserving responsiveness and reducing lag. The global banded solve is O(n)  │
│                          │ per iteration, keeping execution time comparable. Output contract (length and index       │
│                          │ alignment) is identical to the current implementation.                                    │
