╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lower_damper_alpha_floor_with_corr_guard                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The feedback from prior generations shows the alpha floor of 0.55 in the zero-phase       │
│                          │ bilateral damper leaves residual ripple: smoothness is capped around 0.39-0.41 with 24-31 │
│                          │ slope changes and 23-24 false reversals, while lag error (0.33) and responsiveness (0.75) │
│                          │ have headroom. Because the bilateral one-pole is zero-phase, lowering alpha adds no       │
│                          │ direct lag penalty — the only risk is over-flattening genuine dynamics.                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The edit does three things:                                                               │
│                          │ 1. **Lower the alpha floor from 0.55 to 0.40** so heavily-residual-noise regimes get      │
│                          │ substantially more ripple suppression (directly attacking slope_changes, false_reversals, │
│                          │ and smoothness).                                                                          │
│                          │ 2. **Cascade two moderate passes at alpha=0.65 when strong suppression is requested**     │
│                          │ instead of one strong pass below 0.65. Cascade smoothing has a flatter group-delay        │
│                          │ profile near the test signal's dominant frequencies, so it suppresses ripple without the  │
│                          │ passband ripple/edge artifacts a single aggressive one-pole can introduce.                │
│                          │ 3. **Lag-based correlation guard**: after damping, compute the Pearson correlation        │
│                          │ between the damped output and the pre-damper RTS output. If correlation falls below 0.97  │
│                          │ (indicating the damper has distorted the trajectory — i.e., over-smoothing that would     │
│                          │ hurt tracking accuracy and lag error), back alpha off linearly toward 0.85 and re-apply a │
│                          │ single pass. This keeps the multi-objective balance: aggressive smoothing only survives   │
│                          │ where it demonstrably does not harm the signal shape.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ All changes are confined to the damper section of `kalman_rts_filter`; no other functions │
│                          │ or the output contract are touched.                                                       │
