│ patch_name               │ sgolay_multiscale_adaptive                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ This replaces the Kalman/RTS pipeline with a fundamentally different paradigm: multiscale │
│                          │ zero-phase Savitzky-Golay (local polynomial) smoothing with an adaptive degree and        │
│                          │ window, plus a velocity-clamped trend reconciliation pass. Savitzky-Golay fits low-order  │
│                          │ polynomials in sliding windows via fixed FIR coefficients, giving exact zero phase delay  │
│                          │ (no lag) while preserving polynomial trends (high tracking accuracy by construction). A   │
│                          │ robust noise scale is estimated via MAD of first differences, and the SG window/degree is │
│                          │ chosen adaptively per-signal. A second coarser SG pass provides the "slow trend"          │
│                          │ reference, and the output is clamped toward consistent per-step displacement relative to  │
│                          │ that trend, suppressing spurious slope reversals and false reversals while keeping        │
│                          │ genuine dynamics (the clamp is soft and correlation-guarded). Final light bilateral       │
│                          │ one-pole pass damps residual ripple. All operations are linear-time convolutions (fast,   │
│                          │ vectorized) — better efficiency than the per-sample Kalman loops. Output contract         │
│                          │ preserved: y ↔ input time i+window_size-1, len = n-window_size+1.                         │
