│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ local_block_mad_q                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ Make the process noise Q locally adaptive via robust block-MAD statistics rather than a   │
│                          │ single global (or EWMA/recursive) estimate. After the first Kalman+RTS pass produces the  │
│                          │ denoised y1, compute second differences of y1 in overlapping blocks of length ≈           │
│                          │ window_size (50% overlap). Because y1 is already denoised, its d2 reflects genuine        │
│                          │ dynamics (e.g., the 5 Hz component) that a single global Q under-serves. Per block:       │
│                          │ robust σ from MAD, subtract the (σ_r/√w)·√6 propagated noise floor, floor at 0.05·σ_r,    │
│                          │ cap at 1.5·σ_r, then linearly interpolate block values to a per-sample q_k sequence used  │
│                          │ in the second (refinement) pass's prediction step as 0.25·q_k. Busy segments get larger   │
│                          │ process noise (better tracking, lower lag_error and avg_error in oscillatory regions)     │
│                          │ while quiet segments are smoothed harder (fewer slope_changes / false_reversals). Block   │
│                          │ medians are robust to transients, unlike EWMA which chases bursts — the Gen-42            │
│                          │ EWMA-adaptive R scored 0.68 with lag_error 0.34; the robust block counterpart targets the │
│                          │ same objectives without over-trusting noisy segments.                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
