│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ noise_floor_subtracted_two_pass_q                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The recommendation targets lag_error (0.27) and avg_error (0.29): a single global Q       │
│                          │ under-serves the 5 Hz component. Instead of estimating Q from the raw denoised input      │
│                          │ (which mixes measurement noise into the "dynamics"), run the Kalman pass once with a      │
│                          │ conservative base Q, then estimate per-sample Q from the *smoothed* first-pass output's   │
│                          │ second differences. Because the smoothed output's d2 contains far less measurement noise, │
│                          │ its block-MAD reflects genuine local dynamics. We subtract the expected residual noise    │
│                          │ floor per block — the residual noise in a window-mean-like estimate scales as σ_r/√w, and │
│                          │ second differences amplify it by √6 — then floor at 0.05σ_r and cap at 1.5σ_r,            │
│                          │ interpolating to per-sample Q. This gives large Q (fast tracking) in genuinely busy       │
│                          │ segments and small Q (heavy smoothing) in slow segments, directly reducing lag on         │
│                          │ transients while suppressing spurious reversals elsewhere. σ_r is estimated robustly from │
│                          │ the Hampel residual via MAD.                                                              │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0106                                                                                   │
