│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ whittaker_fixed_lambda_polish                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the final Savitzky-Golay polish with a fixed-λ Whittaker–Henderson                │
│                          │ second-difference smoother. The SG polish is a local polynomial fit with a fairly         │
│                          │ ripple-prone passband; a global second-order-difference penalized least squares, min ||z  │
│                          │ − y||² + λ||D₂z||², has a flatter passband and sharper stopband, so it suppresses         │
│                          │ residual ripple (the driver of slope_changes ≈ 97 and false_reversals ≈ 70) without       │
│                          │ adding the group delay a stronger one-pole/polynomial smoother would introduce (lag_error │
│                          │ is already good at 0.27 and must not degrade). The system matrix I + λD₂ᵀD₂ is            │
│                          │ pentadiagonal and symmetric positive definite, so it is solved in O(n) with a banded      │
│                          │ Cholesky (scipy solveh_banded), preserving the ~0.05s execution time; a dense-solve       │
│                          │ fallback keeps it robust when scipy is unavailable.                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The smoothing strength is data-driven but *constant along the signal* (the diagnosed      │
│                          │ failure mode of earlier Whittaker attempts was a curvature-relaxed λ leaking noise where  │
│                          │ the penalty relaxed): λ = clip(25·(r_std/σ_r)², 1, 200), where σ_r is the robust          │
│                          │ residual-noise scale from the existing Hampel residual MAD (recomputed in the driver) and │
│                          │ r_std is the residual scale of the Kalman blend against the denoised input. This adapts   │
│                          │ the polish strength to the actual remaining noise level while staying bounded.            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Critically, the smoother is applied to the *full-length* signal before the Stage-6 anchor │
│                          │ slicing, so the `output_length = n − window_size + 1` window-semantics contract is        │
│                          │ preserved exactly (a length-changing post-smoother was the failure that zeroed an earlier │
│                          │ generation).                                                                              │
