╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ locally_adaptive_q_blocks                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the globally-scalar process noise Q in the second (refinement) RTS pass with a    │
│                          │ locally adaptive, per-sample Q derived from block-MAD second differences of the           │
│                          │ first-pass smoothed output y1. Since y1 is already denoised, its second differences       │
│                          │ reflect genuine local dynamics (e.g., the 5 Hz component) that a global Q under-serves:   │
│                          │ busy segments get larger process noise (less lag, better tracking), quiet segments get    │
│                          │ tighter Q (more smoothing, fewer slope changes/false reversals). Blocks of length ≈       │
│                          │ window_size with 50% overlap, noise floor (σ_r/√w)·√6 subtracted in quadrature, floored   │
│                          │ at 0.05σ_r and capped at 1.5σ_r, linearly interpolated per sample. The second pass uses   │
│                          │ 0.25·Q. The third refinement pass is removed (it added execution time and did not help;   │
│                          │ the local-Q pass subsumes its purpose) while keeping the final zero-phase polish to avoid │
│                          │ the jitter collapse seen in over-smoothed variants.                                       │
