│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ whittaker_fixed_lambda_polish                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the final zero-phase bilateral one-pole damper with a fixed-λ Whittaker–Henderson │
│                          │ polish (second-order difference penalty). Rationale: the one-pole damper, even applied    │
│                          │ bilaterally, has a gently sloping passband that leaves residual ripple (slope_changes     │
│                          │ 20.4, false_reversals 14.2) while its exponential smearing contributes to the lag error   │
│                          │ (0.34). A second-order-difference Whittaker smoother has a much flatter passband and      │
│                          │ sharper stopband: it preserves genuine low-curvature trends nearly untouched while        │
│                          │ strongly suppressing high-curvature noise ripple, and being a global symmetric            │
│                          │ least-squares solve it adds zero phase delay. Critically, λ is held CONSTANT over the     │
│                          │ whole signal (uniform curvature penalty), so no segment gets a "curvature-relaxed"        │
│                          │ penalty through which noise leaks — the failure mode of previous adaptive-λ variants. λ   │
│                          │ is set from the residual ripple-to-noise ratio already available in the function: λ =     │
│                          │ clip(25·(r_std/σ_r)², 1, 200). When the Kalman/RTS output is already clean (r_std ≪ σ_r), │
│                          │ λ is small and the signal passes nearly untouched (dynamics preserved); when ripple       │
│                          │ remains, λ grows and suppresses it. The system is pentadiagonal (I + λ·D2ᵀD2), solved in  │
│                          │ O(n) with scipy's banded symmetric Cholesky (`solveh_banded`, bandwidth 5), with a        │
│                          │ dense-solve fallback — preserving the 0.05s runtime budget. The polish is applied at full │
│                          │ length BEFORE the final slice, so the `len(y) = n − window_size + 1` output contract is   │
│                          │ exactly preserved (the length-changing-post-smoother failure of Gen 23 is avoided).       │
