│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ whittaker_polish_kalman_rts                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The current pipeline (Kalman/RTS two-pass smoother + bilateral one-pole damper) reaches   │
│                          │ 0.72 but residual ripple keeps slope changes at ~20 and false reversals at ~14. The       │
│                          │ one-pole damper's amplitude response leaks noise near its corner. I replace the final     │
│                          │ damper with a fixed-λ Whittaker–Henderson polish: minimize ||z − y||² + λ||D₂z||², where  │
│                          │ a constant λ = clip(25·(σ_r/r_std)², 1, 200) is derived from the robust residual MAD      │
│                          │ already computed. The second-order difference penalty penalizes curvature (not slope), so │
│                          │ linear trends pass unattenuated — zero lag for genuine dynamics — while its flatter       │
│                          │ passband suppresses ripple more uniformly than a one-pole cascade. The system matrix I +  │
│                          │ λDᵀD is symmetric positive-definite pentadiagonal, solved with a custom O(n) banded       │
│                          │ (bandwidth-2) Cholesky factorization, preserving the ~0.05s runtime. The polish is        │
│                          │ applied to the full-length signal before slicing, protecting the len(y) = n − window_size │
│                          │ + 1 contract. Key parameters changed: damper alpha heuristic removed entirely; λ schedule │
│                          │ is a deterministic noise-ratio map; RTS Q/4 refinement and dynamics-aware blend retained  │
│                          │ from the best-scoring configuration.                                                      │
