╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ velocity_clamp_guarded_damper_rts                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ This program builds directly on the best-performing prior variant (combined score 0.72) — │
│                          │ the Kalman/RTS two-pass smoother fused with a zero-phase velocity-aware displacement      │
│                          │ clamp — and adds the guarded cascade ripple damper from the higher-accuracy lineage:      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **First pass**: constant-velocity Kalman filter with robust MAD-based measurement      │
│                          │ noise and a widen-only innovation-adaptive R sequence (bursts widen R, clean segments     │
│                          │ stay at the robust floor), plus the relaxed process-noise tuning (floor 0.05·σ_r, cap     │
│                          │ 0.6·σ_r) that let the smoother track fast dynamics.                                       │
│                          │ 2. **Second pass (Q/4 refinement)**: iterated RTS on the smoothed output reusing the      │
│                          │ adaptive R sequence, producing a sharper minimum-variance estimate and a smoothed         │
│                          │ velocity state `xs[:,1]`.                                                                 │
│                          │ 3. **Dynamics-aware blend** between pass-1 and pass-2 outputs, weighted by the            │
│                          │ acceleration-to-noise ratio so high-dynamics signals retain responsiveness.               │
│                          │ 4. **Velocity-aware displacement clamp** (the key winning element): limits |y−y| ≤        │
│                          │ 2·|velocity| + 0.5·σ_r in a forward sweep plus mirrored backward sweep (zero phase), with │
│                          │ a correlation guard that doubles the slack if the clamp distorts the trajectory (corr <   │
│                          │ 0.97) and falls back to the unclamped signal if all retries fail. This directly attacks   │
│                          │ the dominant composite levers — slope changes and false reversals — while genuine fast    │
│                          │ dynamics pass through because their velocity states are large.                            │
│                          │ 5. **Guarded cascade ripple damper**: the zero-phase bilateral one-pole pass now uses     │
│                          │ cascaded moderate passes (alpha=0.65 twice) when heavy suppression is requested, and a    │
│                          │ correlation-based guard that backs alpha off toward 0.85 if the damper distorts the       │
│                          │ trajectory — smoothing residual ripple (further reducing slope changes) without           │
│                          │ flattening genuine trend dynamics or adding phase lag.                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Together these target the composite score's biggest levers (slope_changes: 31.6→~20,      │
│                          │ false_reversals: 24.4→~14 per the clamp lineage) while the guards preserve the tracking   │
│                          │ accuracy and lag that the pure-clamp variant slightly sacrificed. Output contract is      │
│                          │ unchanged: y ↔ input time i + window_size − 1, len(y) = n − window_size + 1.              │
