│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fix_reversal_damp_flip_elimination                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The `_reversal_damp` with `keep=0.25` shrinks sub-threshold sign flips but preserves      │
│                          │ their sign: `out = out + keep*d` still yields a difference opposite to `prev_d`, so a     │
│                          │ downstream reversal counter still registers a flip. This explains why false_reversals has │
│                          │ been pinned at 14.2 across four different suppressor stacks. The fix is true flip         │
│                          │ elimination: on a detected sub-threshold flip, hold the line (out = out, keep=0) or       │
│                          │ average the two conflicting steps, applied in a forward sweep plus a mirrored backward    │
│                          │ sweep for zero phase. The `prev_d` reference must come from the pre-damp difference (not  │
│                          │ the post-damp zero difference) to avoid zero-stall — with keep=0 the post-step difference │
│                          │ becomes 0, which would kill subsequent flip detection if fed back. We retain the corr >=  │
│                          │ 0.97 guard with a keep=0.5 retry (partial averaging) if pure elimination trips the guard. │
│                          │ The zero-difference steps also slightly reduce slope_changes (fewer direction reversals)  │
│                          │ without touching the genuine large reversals, improving smoothness and false_reversals at │
│                          │ essentially zero cost to lag (flips below threshold are noise-scale, not trend-scale).    │
