│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ post_slice_reversal_damp_with_run_hysteresis                                              │
│ patch_description        │ False reversals (14.20) have been byte-identical across programs with different smoother  │
│                          │ stacks, which strongly suggests the trend-flip suppression is effectively a no-op on the  │
│                          │ signal the metrics actually see: everything is applied pre-slice, and the slicing/edge    │
│                          │ effects re-introduce flips. The fix is to (a) damp slope reversals *after* slicing, (b)   │
│                          │ use a more permissive flip threshold (2.5·MAD step std instead of a tight 1.5·step_std)   │
│                          │ so the pass actually fires, and (c) add hysteresis via a run counter — a candidate        │
│                          │ reversal must persist for 2 consecutive steps before the tracked direction flips, which   │
│                          │ kills single-sample noise spikes while preserving genuine trend turns (which persist      │
│                          │ across samples). Finally, if flips remain after damping, we re-run the guarded Whittaker  │
│                          │ escalation on the sliced output with a stricter correlation guard (0.97), since the       │
│                          │ earlier escalation ran on a different signal than what gets scored. This directly targets │
│                          │ false_reversals and slope_changes without touching lag or accuracy.                       │
