│ patch_name               │ zero_phase_curvature_clamp                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The slope-change count (~20.4) and false reversals (~14.2) have never moved across the    │
│                          │ 0.72 family. The velocity clamp bounds |Δy| but still permits alternating +limit/−limit   │
│                          │ steps, which is precisely how residual slope flips survive all downstream damping. This   │
│                          │ edit adds a guarded zero-phase curvature (second-difference) clamp immediately after the  │
│                          │ velocity clamp, mirroring its proven slack-doubling retry pattern with a corr ≥ 0.97      │
│                          │ guard. It limits |y − 2y + y| to 1.5× the robust MAD scale of the output's own second     │
│                          │ differences, applied in forward+backward mirrored sweeps for zero phase. Alternating      │
│                          │ spurious direction reversals — the direct driver of the slope-change and false-reversal   │
│                          │ metrics — are flattened while genuine smooth turns (curvature within the robust scale)    │
│                          │ pass untouched. The 4-attempt slack-doubling guard protects tracking accuracy and lag, so │
│                          │ correlation/accuracy metrics should not regress.                                          │
