│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ guarded_zero_phase_curvature_clamp                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The one sub-metric that has never moved across the 0.72 family is `slope_changes`         │
│                          │ (~20.4), and the root cause is structural: `_velocity_clamp` bounds the *first*           │
│                          │ difference |Δy| per step, but it still permits *alternating* +limit/−limit steps — a      │
│                          │ zigzag that fully respects the velocity bound yet flips the slope sign at every sample.   │
│                          │ The existing reversal-damp stages cannot fix this because `_reversal_damp` only shrinks   │
│                          │ the magnitude of a sub-threshold flip step (keep·d keeps the same sign), so the flip      │
│                          │ *count* is untouched; only the alpha=0.85 bilateral pass can merge flips, and its corr ≥  │
│                          │ 0.97 guard against the pre-loop signal breaks the escalation early.                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix is to attack the second derivative directly: add `_curvature_clamp`, which limits │
│                          │ the step-to-step *slope change* |y − 2y + y| ≤ curv_limit via a forward sweep             │
│                          │ (extrapolating the newest point) plus a mirrored backward sweep, so the operation is      │
│                          │ zero-phase and adds no lag. Clamping the second difference converts an alternating ±L     │
│                          │ zigzag into a monotone decelerating/increasing glide (slope changes by exactly ±limit per │
│                          │ step, crossing zero once), which is precisely "slope change minimization": spurious       │
│                          │ reversals vanish while each genuine turning point remains exactly one slope change        │
│                          │ (slightly rounded, still zero-phase). The limit is auto-calibrated as 1.5× the robust MAD │
│                          │ scale of the output's own second differences, so it adapts per-signal to the              │
│                          │ smooth-curvature floor; genuine trend curvature sits at/below this scale and passes       │
│                          │ through, while noise zigzags (second differences ~2× the velocity slack) exceed it by a   │
│                          │ wide margin.                                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Distortion safety mirrors the proven velocity-clamp pattern exactly: 4 attempts with      │
│                          │ limit doubling (1.5× → 12× MAD scale), accepting the first candidate whose correlation    │
│                          │ with the pre-clamp signal is ≥ 0.97, otherwise disabling the clamp. Cost is two O(n)      │
│                          │ Python loops per attempt (≤ 4 attempts) — negligible vs the current 0.08 s runtime,       │
│                          │ keeping efficiency_score at 1.0. Expected effect: slope_changes and false_reversals drop  │
│                          │ substantially (the two worst sub-metrics), smoothness rises, while                        │
│                          │ lag_error/correlation/avg_error are protected by the corr ≥ 0.97 guard and the zero-phase │
│                          │ construction. Downstream stages (ripple damper, reversal damp, flip-escalation loop with  │
│                          │ target 12) remain as final polish and will now typically exit immediately.                │
