│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ guarded_curvature_clamp                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Diagnosis: across the entire 0.72 family, slope_changes (20.40), false_reversals (14.20)  │
│                          │ and smoothness_score (0.50) have never moved — velocity clamps, Whittaker polish,         │
│                          │ adaptive-Q passes and reversal damping all bound the *first* difference only.             │
│                          │ `_velocity_clamp` limits each step to |Δy| ≤ 2|v|+slack but permits alternating           │
│                          │ +limit/−limit steps; those alternating-budget steps are pure second-difference            │
│                          │ (curvature) outliers and they are exactly what registers as the ~20 surviving slope       │
│                          │ changes. No current stage constrains curvature.                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Edit: add `_curvature_clamp`, a zero-phase second-difference limiter applied immediately  │
│                          │ after `_velocity_clamp` (before the reversal damp). It clamps |y − 2y + y| to 1.5× the    │
│                          │ robust MAD scale of the *output's own* second differences — self-scaling: genuinely       │
│                          │ curved dynamics set the MAD, so the limit sits above typical true curvature and passes    │
│                          │ it, while ripple riding quiet/straight segments lies far above the MAD and is folded onto │
│                          │ the local linear continuation (alternating ±budget steps become straight runs,            │
│                          │ eliminating their sign flips). The limit is enforced in mirrored forward/backward sweeps  │
│                          │ (forward adjusts the trailing sample k+1, backward adjusts the leading sample k−1), so    │
│                          │ the operation adds zero group delay — lag_error and responsiveness_score are untouched.   │
│                          │ It reuses the proven guard pattern from `_velocity_clamp`: 4 attempts with budget         │
│                          │ doubling, accepting only if corr ≥ 0.97 against the pre-clamp signal, otherwise identity  │
│                          │ fallback — this protects correlation/accuracy on highly curved or adversarial inputs.     │
│                          │ Cost is two O(n) sweeps, the same complexity class as the existing clamps, so             │
│                          │ efficiency_score (~1.0) and execution_time are preserved. Expected effect: slope_changes  │
│                          │ and false_reversals drop, smoothness_score rises, with zero-phase operation keeping       │
│                          │ lag_error, responsiveness, and correlation intact.                                        │
